FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1566 — `find_references` computes `target = analysis.token_binding[index]` and, when it is `None`, matches every unresolved token of that spelling that is not a member/type name; it never checks whether the *requested* token itself is a member or type name. So `find_references("set holder to 1\nsay holder.abs\nsay abs(-1)\n", 2, 12)` on the member `abs` returns the unrelated plain builtin use at `(3,5)` (target is `None`, `(2,12)` is filtered out by `member_names`, `(3,5)` is not), instead of returning nothing / only itself. Same for a type name queried directly. The only test, `tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1836`, queries `(3,5)` (the plain use) rather than `(2,12)` (the member), so it never exposes this. — Fix: if `target.is_none() && (analysis.member_names.contains(&index) || analysis.type_names.contains(&index))`, answer empty (or just the requested location) instead of scanning all same-spelled unresolved names. — Traced through the exact branch at 1558-1576.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1528 — `goto_definition` guards builtin resolution with `!analysis.member_names.contains(&index)` but omits the analogous `!analysis.type_names.contains(&index)`. A type annotation that happens to spell a builtin, e.g. `to total(items of abs)` (the type name is marked in `type_names` by `skip_type_expression`), resolves to `DefinitionKind::Builtin` even though `find_references` in the same file correctly treats it as a type name. `tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1859` only asserts the plain `abs` still resolves; no test asks goto of the type-name position. — Fix: add `&& !analysis.type_names.contains(&index)` to the guard.

3. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:2026 — the field/method same-name test only covers the field-declared-first order. `declare_fresh` (src/lsp.rs:499) inserts with `scope.entry(name).or_insert(id)`, so when the method is declared *before* `has name`, the plain-name entry stays the method and a bare field read resolves to the method, the opposite of `member_binding`'s "bare member prefers the field" rule. This reverse ordering is a new branch with no test on either side; add a fixture with `to can name()` above `has name` and pin the bare-use expectation.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
